{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3060","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-runtime-hints-r7-regroom.md@sha256:c11737aff187a8c7ddcf19eb65152571cba4b7c55f66a605f9b45fc8509d8446"},"owner_reference":"3060","content":"# Architecture Spec: R7-Compliant Runtime Hints in `backlog_view` (re-groom, 2026-09-20)\n\nBacklog item: #3060 \u2014 \"fix: runtime hints steer agents away from paginated navigation\"\n\n**Supersedes**: `plan/architect-runtime-hints-r7.md` (2026-08-20) for implementation purposes. That\ndocument's \u00a72 ordinal-compatibility resolution and \u00a73/\u00a74 diffs for Acceptance Criteria 1, 2 and 4\n(entry 13.3) remain correct and unchanged \u2014 verified against current `origin/main`\n(`d547db370`, 2026-09-20: `_build_compact_manifest`/`_build_over_budget_view` are byte-identical to\nwhat that spec diffs against, confirming the fix is still unmerged). This document does not\nre-derive that work; it recaps it in \u00a72 and adds the three pieces the 2026-09-19 re-groom found\nmissing from both the prior spec and the drafted PR #3649: the AC3 budget-limit wording, the AC5\nschema-description fix, and the AC8 branch/merge strategy. \u00a75 gives the complete, consolidated diff\ntarget (all of AC1/2/3/4/5) so an implementer has one document to build against.\n\n**Revision note (2026-09-20, correction pass):** the first version of this document got two things\nwrong, corrected below against primary sources fetched this pass (`gh api` on the cited issue\ncomments; `backlog_view(selector='#3060', navigate='7.5')` for the cited Impact Radius entry):\n\n1. \u00a77 and ADR 9.3 previously recommended abandoning PR #3649 for a fresh branch off `main`. The\n   repo owner's decision (issue comment 5742560104) explicitly directs the opposite: \"starting\n   from the #3649 branch.\" \u00a77 and ADR 9.3 are rewritten to land on that branch.\n2. \u00a75.4's docstring and ADR 9.1 previously claimed naming `map=True` \"cannot itself violate\" R7\n   prohibition 3. Impact Radius entry 7.5 and the owner's comment 5740080449 (item 2) both record,\n   as fact already established for this item, that the narrowed hint (as drafted on PR #3649,\n   commit `dc5b6d264:server.py:2174-2182`) is a live prohibition-3 violation \u2014 it names `map=`,\n   `navigate=`, `head=`, and `skip_tokens=` in prose while the over-budget response carries no\n   field for any of them. Acceptance Criterion 4 (entry 13.3) records this as accepted, unmet debt\n   pending #3059, not as something this fix resolves. \u00a75.4 and ADR 9.1 are rewritten to state that\n   plainly instead of claiming compliance the record does not grant.\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nAcceptance Criteria (item #3060, entry 13.3, the only non-struck version) now has 8 criteria. Five\nare behavioral changes inside `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`:\n\n| AC | What | Status before this document |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `_hint` leads with section-narrowing, not `summary=False` | Designed (prior spec \u00a73), drafted on PR #3649 |\n| 2 | `_usage` for a narrowed-and-still-over-budget request names `map=True`, not repeated `sections=` advice | Designed (prior spec \u00a74), drafted on PR #3649 |\n| 3 | That same `_usage` states `map=True` is not unconditionally safe on a large item | **Not designed. Closed here (\u00a73).** |\n| 4 | Item bookkeeping records R7 prohibition 3 as unmet for the narrowed hint, pending #3059 | Already true (Dependencies 8.2, Impact Radius 7.5, owner comment 5740080449 item 2) \u2014 no code change; \u00a75.4/\u00a79.1 now state it accurately instead of overclaiming compliance |\n| 5 | `summary` Field description lists a section-scoped read before an unscoped full read | **Not designed. Closed here (\u00a74).** |\n| 6 | `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s ownership table keeps #3060 listed until 1-5, 7, 8 hold | Already true today (row: \"Consumer guidance and contract identity\" names #3060; confirmed again by Impact Radius 7.5 \u2014 PR #3649 excludes the commit that broke this) \u2014 no design work, a close-out check only |\n| 7 | Five named test files exit 0 | Test plan in \u00a76 |\n| 8 | The change is on `origin/main`, not only an open PR | **Not designed. Closed here (\u00a77).** \u2014 land by merging `origin/main` into PR #3649's branch (owner-directed starting point, issue comment 5742560104) and adding AC3/AC5 on top, not a fresh branch |\n\nAll of it stays inside `server.py`. No change to `disclosure_handler.py`, `ordinal_mapper.py`, or\n`content_normalizer.py` \u2014 confirmed again this pass, no new evidence changes that boundary.\n\n## 2. Recap: Ordinal-Compatibility Resolution (unchanged from prior spec)\n\nThe prior spec's \u00a72 answered whether `_build_over_budget_view()`'s narrowed `_usage` may name\n`navigate=<N>` using the `[N]` numbers in its own `sections_index`. Answer: no \u2014 that field is\nbuilt by `_sections_index_from_result()`'s `enumerate(result.sections.items())` fallback\n(server.py:2133), a **dict**-order enumeration, while `navigate=` ordinals are resolved by\n`OrdinalPathMapper` against a **document-order** `[N]`-line parse\n(`ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()`, whose own docstring states the two collapse differently\nunder duplicate section titles). Naming an unverified ordinal would violate R7 prohibition 3\n(\"never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies\") a second time in the same fix.\nThe resolution: name `map=True` instead \u2014 it takes no address argument, so nothing about it can be\nan *invalid* address (this fixes the address-validity problem only; it does not make the hint R7\nprohibition-3 compliant overall \u2014 see \u00a79.1 for the corrected, narrower claim). This is unchanged\nand still the correct mechanism for AC2. See the prior artifact's \u00a72 for\nthe full four-finding evidence chain; it was not re-verified line-by-line this pass because no fact\nin it depends on anything the 2026-09-19 re-groom touched (AC3/AC5/AC8 are additive, not revisions\nof AC1/2/4's mechanism).\n\n## 3. AC3 \u2014 The `map=True` Budget-Limit Statement\n\n### 3.1 What AC3 requires\n\n> The same `_usage` text that recommends `map=True` also states, in its own wording, that a\n> `map=True` call is not guaranteed to stay within the response token budget on a large item \u2014 the\n> hint does not present `map=True` as an unconditionally safe next step, it names the limit.\n\nOwner decision (Dependencies 8.2, RT-ICA Final 2.7 condition 11): the limit is recorded \"in the\n`_usage` hint text itself\" \u2014 not a separate response field, not deferred to #3059.\n\n### 3.2 Resolving Q1 (wording and whether to name #3059)\n\nTwo design choices were open per the re-groom (Q1): word the limit so it is both observable to a\ntest and non-steering, and decide whether to name issue #3059 or name an in-response recourse.\n\n**Decision: name the MAP response's own `over_budget` field, not the issue number.** An agent\nconsuming this hint cannot act on \"wait for #3059 to land\" \u2014 an internal tracker reference is not\nan address or a mechanism it can call, so naming it would add prose with no operational value (and\narguably repeats the R7 prohibition-1 shape this whole fix removes: a suggestion the caller can't\nact on now). What the agent *can* act on: `map=True`'s own response carries an `over_budget`\nboolean (`MapResponse.over_budget`, `disclosure_handler.py:387-403`, confirmed present and\ndiagnostic-only, unchanged by this fix per Anti-Goal 2). Naming that field gives the agent an\nobservable signal to check after making the call \u2014 consistent with the \"what went wrong, what to\nexpect\" shape of actionable tool-response guidance generally (no MCP-specific `backlog_view`\nprecedent for this exact wording exists yet in the codebase; the general principle \u2014 a hint states\nan observable follow-up check, not an untraceable external reference \u2014 is what both R7's existing\ntext and general MCP tool-response guidance agree on, per this pass's supporting web search, cited\nin Research Source Notes below).\n\nAlso resolves Fact-Check gap #1 from the re-groom (`_usage` narrowed text on PR #3649 has no such\nstatement at all) \u2014 this document is the first to specify the wording.\n\n### 3.3 Wording (verbatim, becomes part of `_build_narrowed_usage()` in \u00a75)\n\n```text\nThis response exceeded the 4000-token budget (99999 chars in full form), even after narrowing\nto a single section \u2014 further section= narrowing is not available for this request. Get this\nitem's ordinal map, then page through the oversized section with navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=:\n  backlog_view(selector='#3060', map=True)\nNote: on a large item, that map=True call can itself exceed this budget (check its own\nover_budget field) \u2014 there is no smaller retrieval for this section available yet.\n```\n\nThe added sentence: (a) is present unconditionally in the narrowed branch, so it can never be read\nas \"safe in this case\" \u2014 it always accompanies the `map=True` suggestion; (b) names one\nalready-real field (`over_budget`, part of the MAP response shape today) rather than a value this\nfix would have to invent; (c) makes no claim about *when* a smaller retrieval will exist, only that\nnone does yet \u2014 accurate per AC4/Dependencies 8.2 (pending #3059) without naming #3059 by number.\n\n## 4. AC5 \u2014 `summary` Parameter Schema Description\n\n### 4.1 What AC5 requires\n\n> The `backlog_view` tool's `summary` parameter description ... lists a section-scoped read before\n> an unscoped full-content read \u2014 not full-content first.\n\n### 4.2 Current text (`server.py:2264-2274`, confirmed unchanged on `origin/main` this pass)\n\n```python\nsummary: Annotated[\n    bool,\n    Field(\n        description=(\n            \"When True (default), returns a compact routing manifest with issue_number, title, labels, \"\n            \"status, plan_address, sections_index (all available sections as [N] Title (count) lines), \"\n            \"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load full content or specific sections. \"\n            \"When False, returns the full response unchanged.\"\n        )\n    ),\n] = True,\n```\n\n### 4.3 New text \u2014 one-clause reorder, nothing else changes\n\n```python\nsummary: Annotated[\n    bool,\n    Field(\n        description=(\n            \"When True (default), returns a compact routing manifest with issue_number, title, labels, \"\n            \"status, plan_address, sections_index (all available sections as [N] Title (count) lines), \"\n            \"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load specific sections or full content. \"\n            \"When False, returns the full response unchanged.\"\n        )\n    ),\n] = True,\n```\n\nOnly `\"load full content or specific sections\"` \u2192 `\"load specific sections or full content\"`. No\nmention of `navigate=`/`map=` is added here, for the identical reason \u00a73.3 of the prior spec gives\nfor not adding it to `_hint`: this description's own `sections_index` reference carries the same\ndict-order-vs-document-order ambiguity (\u00a72), and this field never claims an ordinal is valid, so\nadding one here would introduce a claim the description can't back up.\n\n### 4.4 Verified safe \u2014 no pinned test on this string\n\n`grep -rn \"full content or specific sections\" plugins/development-harness` (this pass, 2026-09-20)\nfinds exactly one occurrence: the `server.py:2270` line itself. No test asserts this description\nstring verbatim \u2014 Acceptance Criterion 4/7's named test list (`test_backlog_view_summary_true_*`,\n`test_build_over_budget_view_*`, `_PASSTHROUGH_LEGACY_KEYS`) covers response *field* shape, not the\ntool schema's `Field(description=...)` text. This edit has no test-breakage risk to track.\n\n## 5. Consolidated Diff Target (AC1 + AC2 + AC3 + AC4 bookkeeping + AC5, all in one place)\n\n### 5.1 `_build_compact_manifest()` \u2014 `_hint` (AC1, from prior spec \u00a73, unchanged)\n\n```python\n\"_hint\": (\n    f\"Load specific sections: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='<index, title, or /regex/>')\\n\"\n    f\"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)\"\n),\n```\n\n### 5.2 `_build_over_budget_view()` \u2014 signature (AC2, from prior spec \u00a74.1, unchanged)\n\n```python\ndef _build_over_budget_view(\n    result: _models.ViewItemResult,\n    full_chars: int,\n    selector: str,\n    *,\n    narrowed_to_single_section: bool = False,\n) -> dict[str, object]:\n```\n\nKeyword-only, defaults `False` \u2014 every existing 3-positional-argument call site (including both\ncited unit tests) is unaffected.\n\n### 5.3 Call-site signal computation (AC2, from prior spec \u00a74.2, unchanged)\n\nInside `backlog_view()`, replacing the current unconditional call at `server.py:2513`:\n\n```python\nserialised = _json.dumps(full_response)\nif _view_payload_token_count(full_response) > _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET:\n    narrowed_sections = full_response.get(\"sections\")\n    narrowed_to_single_section = bool(\n        (section is not None or sections_filter is not None)\n        and isinstance(narrowed_sections, dict)\n        and len(narrowed_sections) == 1\n    )\n    return _respond(\n        BacklogViewResponse,\n        _build_over_budget_view(\n            result, len(serialised), selector, narrowed_to_single_section=narrowed_to_single_section\n        ),\n        exclude_none=False,\n        exclude_unset=True,\n    )\n```\n\nUses `full_response[\"sections\"]` (post-`_filter_view_sections()`), not `result.sections` \u2014 the\nplural `sections=[...]` path mutates `full_response` in place but not `result`\n(`_filter_view_sections()` docstring, server.py:1240-1249).\n\n### 5.4 `_build_over_budget_view()` \u2014 `_usage` (AC2 + AC3, updated this pass)\n\n```python\n\"_usage\": (\n    _build_narrowed_usage(selector, full_chars)\n    if narrowed_to_single_section\n    else (\n        f\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \"\n        f\"({full_chars} chars in full form). \"\n        \"Use the sections_index below to identify which sections you need, \"\n        \"then request them individually:\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, sections=['Section Name'])\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='0,1,3')\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='/regex/')\"\n    )\n),\n```\n\n```python\ndef _build_narrowed_usage(selector: str, full_chars: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Build _usage for an over-budget response already narrowed to one section.\n\n    Section-based narrowing (section=/sections=) is already exhausted for the\n    single section that remains \u2014 repeating that advice would violate R7's\n    \"never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted\" prohibition.\n\n    Names map=True, not navigate=<ordinal>: per architect-runtime-hints-r7.md\n    \u00a72, an ordinal drawn from this response's own sections_index is unverified\n    against OrdinalPathMapper.resolve() and could resolve to the wrong\n    content if used. map=True carries no such risk \u2014 the call itself always\n    succeeds. That is the ONLY thing this choice fixes. It does NOT satisfy\n    R7's third prohibition (\"never name a mechanism absent from the response\n    it accompanies\") for this hint: none of map=, navigate=, head=, or\n    skip_tokens= is a field on the over-budget response this text\n    accompanies, and the navigate=/head=/skip_tokens= mechanism is still\n    named in prose below, pointing at the response map=True itself will\n    return. Acceptance Criterion 4 (entry 13.3, backlog item #3060) and the\n    repo owner's 2026-09-19 decision (issue comment 5740080449, item 2)\n    record this as accepted, tracked debt pending #3059 (MAP pagination) \u2014\n    this function does not close that gap, only avoids compounding it with an\n    additionally-unverifiable address.\n\n    States the map=True budget limit unconditionally (AC3): map=True is never\n    presented as guaranteed-safe, and the recourse named (the MAP response's\n    own over_budget field) is something the codebase can already show the\n    caller today, not a forward reference to unshipped work (#3059).\n\n    Args:\n        selector: Original selector string used to build the usage hint.\n        full_chars: Character length of the serialised full response.\n\n    Returns:\n        Usage string naming map=True as the next call, with its budget caveat.\n    \"\"\"\n    return (\n        f\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \"\n        f\"({full_chars} chars in full form), even after narrowing to a single section \u2014 \"\n        \"further section= narrowing is not available for this request. \"\n        \"Get this item's ordinal map, then page through the oversized section with \"\n        \"navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=:\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', map=True)\\n\"\n        \"Note: on a large item, that map=True call can itself exceed this budget \"\n        \"(check its own over_budget field) \u2014 there is no smaller retrieval for this \"\n        \"section available yet.\"\n    )\n```\n\n(As in the prior spec: splitting the narrowed branch into its own function is a readability\nchoice, not a requirement. An inline conditional producing the same string content is equally\nacceptable.)\n\n**On keeping the `navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=` prose (decision, corrected this pass):** the text\nkeeps \"page through the oversized section with navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=:\" \u2014 the exact phrase\nalready on PR #3649 (`dc5b6d264:server.py:2174-2182`, confirmed via `git show`). This document\npreviously claimed that phrase, paired with a bare `map=True` call, avoided an R7 prohibition-3\nviolation; that claim was wrong and is withdrawn (see the Revision Note at the top of this\ndocument). The corrected position: keeping the phrase does not achieve compliance, because\ncompliance is not reachable for this hint at all yet \u2014 Impact Radius 7.5 and owner comment\n5740080449 (item 2) both record that none of `map=`, `navigate=`, `head=`, or `skip_tokens=` is a\nfield on the over-budget response, so naming any of them is already the prohibition-3 exposure AC4\naccepts as open pending #3059. Given that, removing the phrase would not buy any additional\ncompliance \u2014 it would only delete information genuinely useful to the caller once the `map=True`\ncall returns ordinals to navigate with, and it would diverge from PR #3649, the branch the repo\nowner directs this work to start from (issue comment 5742560104). The phrase stays, unchanged from\nwhat #3649 already drafted, for that reason.\n\n### 5.5 `summary` Field description (AC5, new this pass \u2014 \u00a74.3 above, repeated here for diff completeness)\n\n```python\n\"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load specific sections or full content. \"\n```\nreplaces\n```python\n\"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load full content or specific sections. \"\n```\nat `server.py:2270`, no other line in that `Field(...)` block changes.\n\n### 5.6 Substring/field preservation (verifies AC7's named test list stays green)\n\n- `_hint`: `\"summary=False\"` still present (now second line); `{selector}` still present verbatim\n  in both lines \u2014 preserves `test_backlog_view_summary_true_returns_compact_manifest` and\n  `test_backlog_view_summary_true_hint_contains_selector`.\n- Un-narrowed `_usage` branch: byte-identical to current text \u2014 preserves\n  `test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param`.\n- Neither builder adds/removes a top-level key in either branch \u2014 preserves\n  `test_build_over_budget_view_includes_all_nine_fields` and\n  `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`'s `_PASSTHROUGH_LEGACY_KEYS` assertion.\n- `summary` Field description: no test found referencing this string (\u00a74.4) \u2014 nothing to preserve.\n\n## 6. Test Design (AC7 \u2014 test files; AC3 adds one new assertion)\n\nUnit-level, calling the two builder functions directly, matching the existing direct-call\nconvention in `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py`. Tests 6.1 and 6.2\nare unchanged from the prior spec (\u00a75.1, \u00a75.2); 6.3 adds the AC3 assertion; 6.4 is the\nrequest-level test from the prior spec (\u00a75.3), unchanged.\n\n### 6.1 `_build_compact_manifest()` ordering test (AC1)\n\n```python\ndef test_compact_manifest_hint_leads_with_section_narrowing() -> None:\n    \"\"\"_hint's first line must be a section-narrowing call, not summary=False (AC1).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture with a non-empty sections_index / sections dict>\n    manifest = _build_compact_manifest(result, full_response={}, selector=\"#3060\")\n    first_line = manifest[\"_hint\"].split(\"\\n\", 1)[0]\n    unqualified_full_load = \"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='#3060', summary=False)\"\n    assert \"section=\" in first_line\n    assert first_line != unqualified_full_load\n```\n\n### 6.2 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch unit test (AC2)\n\n```python\ndef test_over_budget_view_narrowed_usage_names_map_not_sections() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Narrowed-to-one-section _usage must not repeat sections= advice and must\n    name a resolvable mechanism (AC2).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture>\n    view = _build_over_budget_view(\n        result, full_chars=99999, selector=\"#3060\", narrowed_to_single_section=True\n    )\n    usage = view[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"sections=\" not in usage\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"#3060\" in usage\n    assert \"sections=['\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='0\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='/\" not in usage\n```\n\n### 6.3 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch budget-limit test (AC3, new this pass)\n\n```python\ndef test_over_budget_view_narrowed_usage_states_map_budget_limit() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Narrowed _usage must not present map=True as unconditionally safe (AC3).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture>\n    view = _build_over_budget_view(\n        result, full_chars=99999, selector=\"#3060\", narrowed_to_single_section=True\n    )\n    usage = view[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"over_budget\" in usage\n    assert \"no smaller retrieval\" in usage\n```\n\nThis pins the limit statement literally \u2014 matching the existing convention of pinning exact\nsubstrings elsewhere in this test file (\u00a76.2 above; the prior spec's \u00a73.4/\u00a74.4). If the\nimplementation's exact wording differs from \u00a75.4's, update this assertion to match the\nimplementation's actual chosen phrase, not the other way around: the requirement is that a\nlimit-stating sentence exists, not this specific string.\n\n### 6.4 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch integration test (AC2/AC3 at the request level, required)\n\nAC2 and AC3 are phrased at the request level, and \u00a75.3's predicate is exactly what 6.1-6.3 cannot\nexercise (they bypass `backlog_view()` entirely). Drive the real tool end to end against a fixture\nitem with a section whose own content alone still exceeds `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`:\n\n```python\nasync def test_backlog_view_section_narrowed_over_budget_names_map(monkeypatch) -> None:\n    \"\"\"A section= request that narrows to one section and is still over budget must\n    receive a _usage naming map=True with its budget caveat, not repeated\n    sections=/section= advice (AC2, AC3).\"\"\"\n    response = await backlog_view(selector=\"<fixture selector>\", summary=False, section=\"<one oversized section>\")\n    assert response[\"_over_budget\"] is True\n    usage = response[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"over_budget\" in usage\n    assert \"sections=['\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='0\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='/\" not in usage\n```\n\nIf this test fails to observe `narrowed_to_single_section` evaluating `True` at the call site (no\n`map=True` in the response), \u00a75.3's predicate has a defect and must be corrected \u2014 do not adjust\nthe assertion to match incorrect behavior.\n\n### 6.5 Existing suite (AC7)\n\nNo changes required to any of the 5 files AC7 names beyond adding the tests above:\n`test_backlog_core_server.py`, `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`,\n`test_view_over_budget_gate.py`, `test_view_pagination_section_index.py`,\n`test_section_filter_miss.py`. Per \u00a75.6, every existing substring/field-name assertion in those\nfiles continues to pass unmodified \u2014 confirmed by direct diff review against `origin/main`, not\nmerely by the absence of an obvious break.\n\n## 7. AC8 \u2014 Landing on `origin/main`\n\n### 7.1 The problem\n\nPer Impact Radius 7.5 (re-verified via `git show dc5b6d264:...` and PR metadata) and the owner's\n2026-09-19T14:14:22Z decision comment (issue comment 5742560104): PR #3642 is closed \u2014 its commit\n`7c9203b05` broke AC6 by removing #3060 from `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s ownership table, and\nits message carried a closing keyword a squash merge would have applied. It is **replaced by PR\n#3649** (`fix/3060-r7-hints`), which carries the same two hint/usage commits (`a90ed1e06`,\n`dc5b6d264`) without the ARCHITECTURE.md-breaking commit. #3649 references #3060 and does not close\nit. #3649 is open, `mergeable: CONFLICTING` (per the 2026-09-19 re-groom's `gh pr view`, not\nre-verified this pass \u2014 re-verify before acting on it), and touches all three `plugin.json`\nmanifests (`.claude-plugin/`, `.codex-plugin/`, `.cursor-plugin/`) in addition to\n`backlog_core/server.py` and two test files. Independent of the conflict, #3649's `server.py` diff\nis incomplete against the final 8-criterion Acceptance Criteria \u2014 it has no AC3 or AC5 change, per\nthe table in \u00a71.\n\n### 7.2 Resolution approach (Q4) \u2014 land on PR #3649's branch, not a fresh one\n\n**The repo owner has already decided the starting point.** Issue comment 5742560104: \"The work\nstage runs `dh:add-new-feature` again against the Acceptance Criteria of 2026-09-19 (entry 13.3),\n**starting from the #3649 branch**.\" This document's first version recommended abandoning #3649 for\na fresh branch off `main` \u2014 that contradicted this recorded decision and is withdrawn (see the\nRevision Note at the top of this document). The corrected approach:\n\n1. On branch `fix/3060-r7-hints` (PR #3649), merge `origin/main` into the branch \u2014 a merge commit,\n   not a rebase, and no force-push. The branch is already open and pushed; rewriting its history\n   would invalidate the existing PR's review state for no benefit `git merge` doesn't also give.\n2. Resolve the `plugin.json` conflicts by taking `origin/main`'s side of those three hunks. This\n   does not require guessing a \"correct\" version number: this repo bumps\n   `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`'s version via a pre-commit hook on every commit\n   (`plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md`), so whatever version lands in the merge commit is\n   superseded by the hook on the very next commit \u2014 step 3 below is that next commit. Taking\n   `main`'s side is simply the smaller, mechanical resolution; taking the branch's side would work\n   equally once re-bumped, but has no advantage and more manual diffing to get there.\n3. Resolve any conflicting hunks in `backlog_core/server.py`/the two touched test files by keeping\n   #3649's `_hint` reorder (AC1) and `narrowed_to_single_section` branch (AC2) \u2014 confirmed\n   unchanged and correct in \u00a72/\u00a75.1-5.3 above \u2014 then commit \u00a75.4's AC3 addition (the budget-limit\n   sentence, new relative to #3649) and \u00a75.5's AC5 fix (also new relative to #3649) on top, plus\n   the \u00a76.3/\u00a76.4 tests. This commit is what re-bumps `plugin.json` per step 2.\n4. Push the branch (no force-push \u2014 a fast-forward-compatible push after an ordinary merge commit),\n   letting PR #3649 update in place rather than opening a new PR. #3649 already references #3060\n   without closing it, matching this item's Dependencies 8.2 record; no PR metadata change needed\n   for that.\n\nBefore starting: run `/rebase`'s pre-analysis (or read\n`plugins/development-harness/docs/branch-transfer-preflight.md` directly, per this repo's own\nbranch-transfer preflight rule) to get a per-file disposition of the `main`-vs-branch conflict\nbefore resolving it by feel \u2014 a process instruction for the implementing task, not a substitute for\nthe branch decision above.\n\n**If this approach turns out not to work** (for example: the `plugin.json` conflicts are not\nresolvable by simply taking `main`'s side because the branch's own commits depend on the version\nvalue in a way not yet found in this pass, or the merge otherwise cannot be completed without\nforce-pushing over reviewed history) \u2014 that is a fact to bring back to the repo owner as a concern\nwith evidence, not a reason to silently fall back to a fresh branch. This document does not\npre-authorize that fallback.\n\n### 7.3 What AC8 verifies, mechanically\n\n`git log origin/main --oneline | grep <merge commit>` (or equivalent) once PR #3649 merges. Not\nsatisfied by an open PR, a local branch, or a plan reporting 100% complete with nothing pushed \u2014\nthis is the exact failure mode AC8 exists to catch, per its own text and per this item's\nDependencies 8.2 provenance (plan `Pe6bcc5c2` previously reported complete with nothing merged).\n\n## 8. Module Boundaries (unchanged \u2014 confirms Impact Radius Files table)\n\n| File | This fix | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| `backlog_core/server.py` | **Change target** | Both hint-construction functions, the call site, and the `summary` Field description live here |\n| `backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` | Read-only reference | `map=True`/`next_call` pattern already correct; not touched |\n| `backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` | Read-only reference | Consulted only to prove \u00a72's ordinal-compatibility finding |\n| `backlog_core/content_normalizer.py` | Read-only reference | Same as above |\n| `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` | Not touched | States R7; no wording change needed |\n| `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Not touched by this fix | AC6 requires #3060 to *stay* listed in the ownership table until AC1-5/7/8 hold \u2014 removing the row is a follow-up close-out action once the fix is verified merged, not part of this diff |\n\n## 9. ADRs\n\n### 9.1 `map=True`, never `navigate=<N>`, in the narrowed `_usage` \u2014 corrected this pass\n\n**Decision**: `_build_over_budget_view()`'s narrowed-branch `_usage` names `map=True`, never\n`navigate=<N>`. **Context**: two independently implemented section-numbering schemes coexist in\n`backlog_core/` (\u00a72) and are proven non-equivalent under duplicate section titles \u2014 an ordinal\ndrawn from `sections_index` is unverified and could resolve to the wrong content.\n**Alternatives rejected**: giving `_build_over_budget_view()` its own `OrdinalPathMapper` build\n(duplicates `map=True`'s own code path, violates Anti-Goals 2/3); naming `navigate=` only when\nindependently verified resolvable (same cost, no benefit over calling `map=True` directly).\n**Consequence**: `map=True` is guaranteed to be a *valid, callable* mechanism, unlike an unverified\n`navigate=<N>`. **This decision does not, and is not claimed here to, satisfy R7's third\nprohibition** (\"never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies\") \u2014 this document's\nfirst version claimed it did; that was wrong and is withdrawn. Per Impact Radius entry 7.5 and the\nrepo owner's 2026-09-19 decision (issue comment 5740080449, item 2), none of `map=`, `navigate=`,\n`head=`, or `skip_tokens=` is a field on the over-budget response this hint accompanies, so naming\nany of them \u2014 including `map=True` \u2014 is the prohibition-3 exposure Acceptance Criterion 4 (entry\n13.3) records as accepted, unmet debt pending #3059. Choosing `map=True` over `navigate=<N>` fixes\nthe *address-validity* problem (\u00a72); it does not and cannot fix the *field-absence* problem, which\nstays open until #3059 gives the over-budget response an actual `map=`/`navigate=` field to point\ninto.\n\n### 9.2 Naming `over_budget`, not `#3059`, as the AC3 recourse (new this pass)\n\n**Decision**: the AC3 limit sentence names the MAP response's own `over_budget` field as the\nobservable follow-up check, not issue #3059 by number. **Context**: Q1 left this open; both\noptions satisfy AC3's literal text. **Alternatives rejected**: naming `#3059` \u2014 an issue number is\nnot something the calling agent can act on inside its own tool-call loop, and referencing unshipped\nwork risks reading as a second prohibition-1-shaped suggestion (\"wait\" is not an action available\nnow). **Consequence**: the hint stays self-contained \u2014 every claim it makes is checkable against\ndata the caller already has or the very next response it will receive, with no dependency on the\nreader knowing this repository's issue tracker.\n\n### 9.3 Merge `origin/main` into PR #3649's branch, then add AC3/AC5 \u2014 corrected this pass\n\n**Decision**: land AC1/2/3/4/5 by merging `origin/main` into `fix/3060-r7-hints` (PR #3649, a\nmerge commit, no force-push), resolving the `plugin.json` conflicts by taking `main`'s side, then\nadding the AC3/AC5 commits on top of that merge. **This document's first version decided a fresh\nbranch off `main` instead, closing PR #3649 \u2014 that directly contradicted the repo owner's recorded\ndecision** (issue comment 5742560104: \"starting from the #3649 branch\") and is withdrawn; see the\nRevision Note at the top of this document. **Context**: #3649 is `CONFLICTING` and, independent of\nthat, missing AC3/AC5 \u2014 new commits are needed regardless of which branch they land on, and the\nowner has already named which branch. **Alternatives rejected**: a fresh branch (contradicts the\nrecorded decision, discussed above); hand-resolving the `plugin.json` conflict by picking a\nspecific version number (unnecessary \u2014 the pre-commit hook recomputes it on the very next commit,\nwhich this plan already makes, so taking either side mechanically and letting the hook re-bump is\nequivalent and simpler than reasoning about which number is \"correct\"). **Consequence**: PR #3649\nstays the one open PR for this fix, its existing review state and its two correct commits\n(`a90ed1e06`, `dc5b6d264`) are preserved, and the final diff against `origin/main` still reflects\nall 8 criteria at once by the time the merge lands.\n\n## Research Source Notes\n\n- Prior architect artifact's own `resource_url`/`github_url` frontmatter fields: none present (it\n  is a plain markdown artifact with no such frontmatter) \u2014 no upstream fetch was applicable for\n  that artifact.\n- Feature-context regroom artifact: same \u2014 no `resource_url`/`github_url` frontmatter fields\n  present; all of its citations are to in-repo files and in-repo issue/PR references, already\n  re-verified directly against `origin/main` source in this document (\u00a71 table, \u00a72, \u00a74.4, \u00a75.6)\n  rather than through an external URL.\n- General MCP tool-response actionability practice (\u00a73.2's supporting citation) was checked via web\n  search this pass, since no in-repo or upstream-URL source existed for that specific claim:\n  actionable MCP error/hint text follows a \"what happened, what to expect, how to check/retry\"\n  shape rather than an unfalsifiable or unreferenceable claim \u2014 consistent with, not contradicting,\n  R7's existing text. Source: aggregated search results, 2026-09-20 (GitHub PR/issue discussions\n  and MCP tutorial sites on tool-error design; no single canonical spec page was cited by name in\n  the search re